      <section class="challenge-section challenge-section--light section-pad" id="open-results" aria-labelledby="open-results-title">
        <div class="container section-grid">
          <div class="section-intro">
            <p class="eyebrow section-label"><span>05 /</span> Open outcomes, shared credit</p>
            <h2 id="open-results-title">Open by design.<br />Credited with care.</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="challenge-policy">
            <h3>Open licenses from the outset.</h3>
            <p>Institutions and contributors must agree <strong>before a challenge begins</strong> that all publishable challenge outputs—code, analyses and notebooks, shareable datasets, model artifacts, documentation, and reports—will be released under appropriate open licenses. Use established open-source licenses for code, openly reusable licenses for data and documentation, and explicitly reusable licenses for models and weights.</p>
            <p>Privacy, research ethics, consent, and third-party rights still apply. Restricted or identifying source data must not be published; challenges should use lawfully shareable derivatives, aggregated data, or synthetic examples where feasible. If these conditions cannot be met, we would rescope or decline the open challenge.</p>
            <h3>Scientific publications belong in the open.</h3>
            <p>If a challenge produces an article, it should be published <strong>open access</strong> or accompanied by a legally shareable <strong>open-access preprint</strong>, respecting the venue's policies and required approvals.</p>
            <p>All contributors should be properly credited. Everyone whose scholarly contribution meets recognized authorship criteria should be invited to be a coauthor and participate in manuscript approval; other contributions should be clearly acknowledged. Authorship and contribution roles are discussed early and reviewed fairly—not used as a participation reward.</p>
            <h3>Results that can be evaluated.</h3>
            <p>A useful outcome could be a reproducible study, a validated analysis, an open scientific tool, a data product, or a well-documented negative result. The final record should identify limitations, relevant evidence, contributors, and whether any real-world adoption has actually been demonstrated.</p>
